RT64 4337374 + plume d890ac8: Metal partial clears must not restore the command-list state cache (found on the THPS1 name-entry screen).

plume's MetalCommandList::clearColor / clearDepthStencil with clear rects draw a quad: that changes what the encoder really has
bound (pipeline, viewport, scissor, depth/stencil state, cull mode, buffer + bytes slot 0). Afterwards it did `stateCache =
previousCache`, i.e. it told plume the OLD values were still bound. The next draw that used the same pipeline as before the
clear then skipped setRenderPipelineState and ran with the clear pipeline: its geometry vanished. In THPS1 every slot of the
name-entry row is "fill rect (underscore), then glyph texrects", so only the first letter showed. Fix: reset the cache (as
endActiveRenderEncoder does) so the next draw binds everything again. Metal only; D3D12 and Vulkan clear natively.

diff --git a/src/contrib/plume/plume_metal.cpp b/src/contrib/plume/plume_metal.cpp
--- a/src/contrib/plume/plume_metal.cpp
+++ b/src/contrib/plume/plume_metal.cpp
@@ -2858,8 +2858,13 @@
 
         activeRenderEncoder->popDebugGroup();
 
-        // Restore previous state if we had one
-        stateCache = previousCache;
+        // [thps-recomp] The quad clear changed what the encoder really has bound (pipeline, viewport, scissor, depth/stencil,
+        // cull mode, buffer and bytes slots 0). Restoring the pre-clear cache claimed the old values were still bound, so the next
+        // draw that used the same pipeline as before the clear never re-set it and ran with the clear pipeline: its geometry
+        // vanished (THPS1: letters on the name-entry screen after a fill rect). Forget the cache; setAll() makes the next draw bind
+        // everything again.
+        (void)previousCache;
+        stateCache.reset();
         dirtyGraphicsState.setAll();
     }
 
@@ -2957,8 +2962,9 @@
 
             activeRenderEncoder->popDebugGroup();
 
-            // Restore previous state if we had one
-            stateCache = previousCache;
+            // [thps-recomp] Same as clearColor: the cache must be forgotten, not restored (see there).
+            (void)previousCache;
+            stateCache.reset();
             dirtyGraphicsState.setAll();
         }
     }
